{"causes": ["The ongoing rivalry between the three nascent Korean kingdoms necessitated continuous military action and strategic expansion.", "The Seok clan's established claim to the Silla throne provided Jobun with the legitimacy to rule and pursue expansionist policies.", "Silla's need for fertile lands and strategic resources, particularly iron from the Gaya region, drove its westward military campaigns."], "effects": ["Jobun's military campaigns weakened the Gaya confederacy, paving the way for Silla's eventual annexation of its territories.", "His successful defense against Goguryeo incursions solidified Silla's northern borders and enhanced its military reputation.", "The consolidation of territory under Jobun's reign contributed to the strengthening of Silla's centralized administration and economic power."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "beolhyu-isageum", "sourceName": "Beolhyu Isageum", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "jobun-of-silla", "targetName": "Jobun of Silla", "context": "Jobun of Silla, as the grandson of Beolhyu Isageum, inherited a political and dynastic legacy that shaped his early rule."}, {"sourceSlug": "jobun-of-silla", "sourceName": "Jobun of Silla", "verb": "CAUSES", "targetSlug": "silla-gaya-wars", "targetName": "Silla-Gaya Wars", "context": "Jobun of Silla initiated significant military campaigns against the Gaya confederacy in 231 CE, escalating the long-standing Silla-Gaya Wars."}, {"sourceSlug": "jobun-of-silla", "sourceName": "Jobun of Silla", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "korean-peninsula", "targetName": "Korean Peninsula", "context": "The reign of Jobun of Silla and his military campaigns primarily occurred within the geopolitical landscape of the Korean Peninsula during the Three Kingdoms period."}, {"sourceSlug": "goguryeo", "sourceName": "Goguryeo", "verb": "COLLABORATES_WITH", "targetSlug": "silla", "targetName": "Silla", "context": "Jobun of Silla's reign saw both conflict and occasional strategic interaction between Silla and the northern kingdom of Goguryeo, particularly in defense and territorial disputes."}, {"sourceSlug": "jobun-of-silla", "sourceName": "Jobun of Silla", "verb": "SUCCEEDS", "targetSlug": "naehae-isageum", "targetName": "Naehae Isageum", "context": "Jobun of Silla succeeded Naehae Isageum as the eleventh monarch of Silla in 230 CE, continuing the Seok clan's rule."}], "places": [{"name": "Gyeongju, South Korea", "role": "Capital City"}, {"name": "Nakdong River Basin, South Korea", "role": "Military Conquest Zone"}, {"name": "Gaya, South Korea", "role": "Rival Confederacy"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Jobun of Silla", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "jobun-of-silla"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q485125", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jobun_of_Silla"], "tags": ["Monarchs & Rulers", "political-systems", "empire-and-colonialism", "geopolitical-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Jobun of Silla's reign was significant for his successful military expansion into the Gaya region and his defense against Goguryeo, which strengthened Silla's territorial integrity and set the stage for its eventual unification of the Three Kingdoms.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-09-01T20:18:24Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#33554174932", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Jobun, also known by his title Jobun Isageum, was the eleventh king of the Korean state of Silla. He was the grandson of Beolhyu Isageum, and a member of the Seok clan. He was the son of Goljeong with Lady Ongmo, a daughter of Kim Gudo. Lady Ongmo's brother was Michu Isageum.", "newValue": "Jobun Isageum, the eleventh monarch of the ancient Korean kingdom of Silla, reigned from 230 to 247 CE, a pivotal period in the Three Kingdoms era marked by intense inter-state rivalry and the consolidation of royal power. A member of the Seok clan, Jobun's reign was characterized by a strategic expansion of Silla's influence, particularly against its western neighbor, Gaya, and the northern kingdom of Goguryeo, laying groundwork for future territorial gains and the eventual unification of the peninsula. His rule represents a critical phase in Silla's early development, transitioning from a tribal confederacy to a centralized state, solidifying the monarch's authority and administrative structures.

During Jobun's seventeen-year reign, Silla engaged in multiple military campaigns, primarily aimed at securing its western borders. In 231 CE, Jobun launched a significant offensive against the Gaya confederacy, capturing its capital and consolidating Silla's control over the fertile Nakdong River basin, a key agricultural and strategic area. This campaign significantly weakened Gaya's independence and brought more territory and resources under Silla's direct administration. Furthermore, Jobun faced and repelled incursions from Goguryeo in 233 CE and 245 CE, demonstrating Silla's growing military capacity and defensive prowess against a formidable northern power.

Jobun's strategic military successes and the subsequent integration of conquered territories contributed to the strengthening of Silla's internal governance and economic base. The expansion into Gaya territories provided Silla with access to crucial iron resources and productive agricultural lands, fueling its continued growth and laying the foundation for its eventual dominance over the peninsula. His reign also saw the continued development of the Silla royal line, with his successor, Gyeore Isageum, continuing the Seok clan's rule, cementing a dynastic stability that would endure for centuries."}, {"timestamp": "2026-09-01T20:18:24Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#33554174932", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Classical era: flourishing of philosophical inquiry, imperial expansion, and codification of la", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "Classical"}, {"title": "Dynastic succession and sovereign authority traditions", "type": "Idea", "year": ""}], "newValue": ["The ongoing rivalry between the three nascent Korean kingdoms necessitated continuous military action and strategic expansion.", "The Seok clan's established claim to the Silla throne provided Jobun with the legitimacy to rule and pursue expansionist policies.", "Silla's need for fertile lands and strategic resources, particularly iron from the Gaya region, drove its westward military campaigns."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-09-01T20:18:24Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#33554174932", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Established dynastic legacies and territorial boundaries", "type": "Idea", "year": "247 CE"}, {"title": "Contributed to enduring intellectual and institutional frameworks shaping subsequent civilizati", "type": "Movement", "year": "247 CE"}], "newValue": ["Jobun's military campaigns weakened the Gaya confederacy, paving the way for Silla's eventual annexation of its territories.", "His successful defense against Goguryeo incursions solid